  "content ":"<p class="lead-paragraph">"Walking to school, the bag fell." Read that sentence again — did the bag walk to school? Grammatically, that's exactly what it says, and this exact trap costs SSC CGL and Bank PO aspirants marks every single year. Dangling participles are one of the few grammar errors that can sound almost correct out loud, which is precisely why textbooks fail to prepare students for spotting them under exam pressure.</p>

<div style="background:#f0f7ff;border-left:4px solid #1B3A6B;padding:15px 18px;margin:20px 0;border-radius:4px;">
<p style="margin:0;"><strong>Quick Answer:</strong> A dangling participle happens when a participle phrase at the start of a sentence has no logical subject to attach to, so it accidentally modifies the wrong noun — like a bag "walking" instead of a person. Fixing it means making sure the subject right after the comma is the one actually performing the action.</p>
</div>

