<h2 id="the-problem">Why Dangling Participles Feel Harder Than They Are</h2>
<p>Most students can define a participle without trouble — the "-ing" or "-ed" form used as an adjective. What breaks down is checking whether that participle phrase actually has a subject to modify, especially when the sentence "sounds fine" when read quickly.</p>
<ul>
<li>You read the sentence in your head and it feels natural, because Indian English speech often drops the logical subject entirely</li>
<li>You focus on the main clause grammar and forget to check what the opening participle phrase is actually describing</li>
<li>You confuse a dangling participle with a misplaced modifier, which needs a different fix</li>
<li>You've never been taught the simple subject-check test, so you're guessing by "feel" under exam pressure</li>
<li>I've watched strong students lose marks here for years — not because they didn't know participles, but because they never learned to ask "who or what is doing this action?" before reading the rest of the sentence</li>
</ul>
<p>But here's what most people get wrong: they try to fix dangling participles by rewording the main clause, when the real fix is almost always about which noun sits immediately after the comma.</p>

<h2 id="common-mistakes">3 Mistakes That Cost the Most Marks</h2>

<p><strong>1. Assuming the participle attaches to the nearest noun automatically.</strong><br>In "Walking to school, the bag fell," the participle phrase "Walking to school" grammatically attaches to "the bag" simply because it's the next noun in the sentence — regardless of what the writer intended.</p>

<p><strong>2. Missing dangling participles hidden mid-sentence.</strong><br>Not every dangling participle sits at the very start. In longer, compound sentences, a participle phrase can dangle in the middle, and students often only scan the opening words of a sentence for errors.</p>

<p><strong>3. Confusing correction with simple rewording.</strong><br>Some students try to fix the error by rearranging words without adding the missing subject, which often creates an equally wrong or awkward sentence instead of a correct one.</p>

