<h2 id="the-solution">The Complete Dangling Participle Fix, Step by Step</h2>
<p>Instead of relying on how a sentence sounds, use one mechanical test every single time: identify the participle phrase, then check whether the very next noun after the comma could logically perform that action.</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="exam-table">
<tr><th>Participle Phrase</th><th>Wrong Sentence</th><th>Corrected Sentence</th></tr>
<tr><td>Walking to school</td><td>Walking to school, the bag fell.</td><td>Walking to school, she dropped her bag.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Having finished the exam</td><td>Having finished the exam, the hall felt empty.</td><td>Having finished the exam, the students left the hall.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Looking out the window</td><td>Looking out the window, the mountains looked beautiful.</td><td>Looking out the window, I saw the beautiful mountains.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Excited about the results</td><td>Excited about the results, the news spread quickly.</td><td>Excited about the results, the students spread the news quickly.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Step 1: Identify the Participle Phrase First</h3>
<p>Underline any phrase starting with an -ing or -ed form at the beginning of a sentence, before reading the rest of it.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>Running late, the bus was missed.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>Running late, <strong>he</strong> missed the bus.</em> (the person running must appear right after the comma)</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 2: Ask "Who or What Is Doing This?"</h3>
<p>Before reading the rest of the sentence, decide who logically performs the action in the participle phrase. That noun must be the subject of the main clause.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>Having studied all night, the exam felt easy.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>Having studied all night, <strong>she</strong> found the exam easy.</em> (the exam cannot "study")</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 3: Check the Very Next Noun After the Comma</h3>
<p>This is the fastest exam-time test — if the noun immediately following the comma cannot logically perform the participle's action, the sentence is wrong.</p>

<h3>Step 4: Fix by Repositioning the Correct Subject, Not by Rewording</h3>
<p>The fix is almost never about changing vocabulary — it's about placing the logical subject directly after the comma.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>Feeling exhausted, the journey seemed endless.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>Feeling exhausted, <strong>they</strong> found the journey endless.</em> (the journey cannot "feel")</div>
</div>

<h3>Step 5: Watch for Dangling Participles Mid-Sentence Too</h3>
<p>Not all errors sit at the start — a participle phrase after a comma anywhere in a compound sentence needs the same subject check.</p>

<div class="table-responsive">
<table class="exam-table">
<tr><th>Signal Phrase</th><th>What to Check</th></tr>
<tr><td>Having + past participle (Having finished, Having seen)</td><td>Subject right after comma must be the one who finished/saw</td></tr>
<tr><td>-ing phrase at sentence start (Walking, Reading, Looking)</td><td>Subject right after comma must be the one walking/reading/looking</td></tr>
<tr><td>Past participle phrase (Excited, Shocked, Convinced)</td><td>Subject right after comma must be the one feeling that state</td></tr>
</table>
</div>

<h3>Step 6: Practice Sentence-Correction Questions With This Exact Pattern</h3>
<p>SSC CGL and Bank PO test this almost exclusively through sentence correction and error spotting — never in isolation as a definition question.</p>
<div class="rule-example-box">
<div class="example-wrong">❌ <em>Arriving late to the meeting, the presentation had already started.</em></div>
<div class="example-correct">✅ <em>Arriving late to the meeting, <strong>he</strong> found the presentation had already started.</em> (the presentation cannot "arrive")</div>
</div>

