<div class="faq-section-block">
<h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h3>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q1. What exactly is a dangling participle?</div><div class="faq-answer">A dangling participle is a participle phrase, usually at the start of a sentence, that has no logical subject to attach to — so it accidentally modifies the wrong noun in the sentence.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q2. How is a dangling participle different from a misplaced modifier?</div><div class="faq-answer">A misplaced modifier has a valid subject somewhere in the sentence but is positioned incorrectly. A dangling participle has no valid subject present at all, so the sentence needs a new subject added, not just repositioned.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q3. Why do dangling participles sound correct when read aloud?</div><div class="faq-answer">Spoken English often drops the logical subject entirely, so sentences with dangling participles feel natural in conversation even though they're grammatically incorrect in writing.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q4. Do dangling participles only appear at the start of a sentence?</div><div class="faq-answer">No — they most commonly appear at the start, but a participle phrase after a comma anywhere in a compound sentence can also dangle if it lacks a logical subject.</div></div>
<div class="faq-item"><div class="faq-question">Q5. What's the fastest way to check for a dangling participle during the exam?</div><div class="faq-answer">Identify the participle phrase, then check whether the very next noun after the comma could logically perform that action. If it can't, the sentence has a dangling participle.</div></div>
</div>

